  "teaId": "martha-mary",
  "text": "Two sisters, one house, and one of them is about to get publicly told, and it is not who you think.\n\nJesus comes into a village. Martha receives him into her house. Her house. She's the host, this was her call.\n\nHer sister Mary sits down at his feet and just, listens.\n\nAnd Martha is what the text calls cumbered about much serving. Cumbered. Dragged in every direction. Because hosting is real work, and she is doing all of it, alone, while her sister sits on the floor.\n\nSo Martha does what a lot of us do. She doesn't go to Mary. She goes over her head.\n\n\"Lord, dost thou not care that my sister hath left me to serve alone? Bid her therefore that she help me.\"\n\nShe's not asking. She's telling him to sort out her sister.\n\nAnd here's where everybody expects a scolding.\n\nHe says, \"Martha, Martha.\"\n\nHer name. Twice. That is not how you open a telling-off. That is how you talk to somebody you love who is drowning.\n\n\"Thou art careful and troubled about many things. But one thing is needful, and Mary hath chosen that good part, which shall not be taken away from her.\"\n\nRead what he did not say. He never tells Mary to get up. He never says the serving was wrong.\n\nHe tells Martha she's allowed to sit down too.",
